  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical findings and threshold relationships are derived from a single, highly dense tropical city-state, which may limit direct application to different climate zones or urban forms.",
      "what": "This study models and compares land surface temperature (LST) at 30-meter resolution and the universal thermal climate index (UTCI) at 1-meter resolution across Singapore. Using a geographically weighted XGBoost and generalized additive model workflow, it evaluates how 2D and 3D urban morphology factors influence both metrics, achieving test R-squared values of 0.855 for LST and 0.905 for UTCI.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that LST is an inadequate proxy for actual human heat stress because it fails to capture shading-driven and radiative processes, showing specifically that sky view factor dominates UTCI variability but has little effect on LST, and that higher albedo is unexpectedly associated with increased UTCI."
    }
